Review and replace this timeout wrapper. Supply TypeScript code and tests with fake timers or an explicit deterministic test clock.

Required API: withTimeout<T>(work: (signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<T>, ms: number, outer?: AbortSignal): Promise<T>.
Requirements: reject negative/nonfinite ms before starting work; an already-aborted outer signal prevents work from starting; otherwise work starts once with a fresh signal. Resolve/reject with work's result if it wins. Timeout or outer abort must abort the supplied signal and reject the wrapper with an Error carrying the corresponding reason. All terminal paths remove listeners and cancel timers. Later work settlement must not produce an unhandled rejection. Handle work throwing synchronously. Define simultaneous settlement ordering through the actual event order rather than polling. Do not assume cancellation stops the underlying operation.

Current sketch:
    export function withTimeout<T>(work: () => Promise<T>, ms: number) {
      return Promise.race([
        work(),
        new Promise<T>((_, reject) => setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('timeout')), ms)),
      ]);
    }

The bug report says completed work leaves timers alive and an outer cancellation is ignored. Keep the replacement small enough to review.

Design a safe SQLite migration and its verification queries. Explain any assumptions explicitly.

Existing tables:
    CREATE TABLE projects(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL);
    CREATE TABLE notes(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, project_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES projects(id), slug TEXT NOT NULL, body TEXT NOT NULL);

The application now requires uniqueness of (project_id, slug), matching slug bytes exactly. Existing data may contain duplicates. Do not discard or merge notes automatically, alter note IDs, or change body/slug text. The migration should report duplicate groups and refuse to install the new constraint when any exist. When none exist, install the uniqueness rule atomically. Existing inserts may run concurrently, so a separate preflight query is insufficient protection. The caller owns the connection and enables foreign-key enforcement. Give a concrete execution sequence, transaction/error handling pseudocode, SQL, rollback behavior, and compact test cases including duplicates across different projects and a write racing the preflight. Avoid an unnecessary full table rebuild.
